# Everything in this folder is published. Nothing private goes here.

# The one file here that is about the repo rather than part of the site. It is
# written for whoever works on the code next, so it names the traps, the things
# that have already gone wrong, and the material that has to stay off the page -
# which makes it the single worst file to serve. It also has to be free to say
# what is true rather than what is publishable, and those are different
# documents. Listed first because it is the one most likely to be added by
# accident: it sits at the root, git offers it on every `git add .`, and a
# README is the file people assume a repo is supposed to have.
README.md

# markdown-pdf and its PhantomJS binary: ~32 MB, used only to rebuild the CV
# PDFs locally. The PDFs themselves are committed, so the site never needs it.
cv/node_modules/

# OS noise
Thumbs.db
Desktop.ini
.DS_Store

# Python bytecode from tools/*.py - regenerated on every run, never published
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# The stats dashboard. It reads the Supabase project, so it is the one file
# here that is about the site rather than part of it - and it is the only place
# the ratings are ever visible, which is exactly why it must not ship. Opened
# from disk, never from the site.
stats.local.html

# flag-icons (MIT, lipis/flag-icons), fetched by hand for the dashboard above.
# 271 flags in two ratios plus their CSS and tooling, around 5 MB, and nothing
# the published site draws uses any of it - the one page that does is itself
# ignored four lines up. The whole folder rather than flags/ alone, because
# the rest is a package.json, a README and a GitHub workflow for a project
# this repo is not building, and a half-ignored vendor folder is how those
# end up committed one file at a time.
assets/flag-icons/
